.TH SETBUF 3
.SH NAME
setbuf \- assign buffering to a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

setbuf(stream, buf)
FILE *stream;
char *buf;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B setbuf
makes
.I stream
use the array
.I buf
of BUFSIZ characters as its buffer, in place of the one the library
would take from
.BR malloc (3).
If
.I buf
is NULL, the stream has no buffer at all, and each character is read or
written by a system call of its own. setbuf is called after the stream
is opened and before it is first read or written.
.PP
A stream given a buffer by setbuf is fully buffered, even on a
terminal: its output is written only when the buffer is full, or is
flushed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	static char buf[BUFSIZ];

	setbuf(stdout, buf);	/* fully buffered, even to a terminal */
	setbuf(stdout, NULL);	/* or not buffered at all */
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
fopen(3), fclose(3), getc(3), putc(3), malloc(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B setbuf
function first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH BUGS
A buffer that is a local variable of a function is gone when the
function returns, while the stream still uses it: close the stream
first, or make the buffer static. This holds for
.B main
too, since
.BR exit (2)
flushes the streams after main has returned.
